{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: API Rate Limiting for LLM Endpoints\n\n## Problem\nThe application currently exposes LLM-backed endpoints (such as `src/routes/sessions.js` invoking pipeline steps in `src/llm/`) without any request rate controls. As explicitly highlighted in `README.md` (\"No rate-limiting is implemented — add it before a hosted deployment\"), any client can submit high-frequency requests, directly threatening cost stability by running up external provider bills (Gemini, OpenAI, or Anthropic) and potentially exhausting upstream API quotas.\n\n## Goals\n- Protect LLM-backed `/api` endpoints against rapid automated hammering and denial-of-wallet attacks.\n- Standardize client error communication by returning HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) accompanied by an informative `Retry-After` header.\n- Implement a lightweight, low-friction solution to deploy quickly without requiring new external cloud infrastructure.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Rate limiting static asset delivery or documentation routes (e.g., `public/index.html`). Rate limiting will be strictly scoped to API routes.\n- Token-based or budget-based cost metering in the initial release (metering requests rather than LLM token counts).\n- Tiered client rate limits or user account subscription tiers.\n\n## User Impact\n- **Standard Users:** Normal users interacting through the UI will experience no disruption under typical usage patterns.\n- **High-Frequency / Automated Clients:** Abusive or runaway clients will receive a clean HTTP 429 response status with a `Retry-After` header indicating when requests can safely resume, preventing unhandled gateway drops or downstream silent failures.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- What exact request volume threshold and sliding window should be enforced (e.g., 20 requests per 15-minute window per client)?\n- Is request-count throttling sufficient for initial cost protection, or do we require immediate token-usage monitoring to guard against large prompt payloads?\n- Under shared network environments (e.g., NAT or corporate VPNs), what is the acceptable risk of collateral throttling across multiple users sharing a public IP?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: API Rate Limiting Implementation\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Middleware Integration:** Introduce an Express-compatible rate-limiting middleware (such as `express-rate-limit`) scoped specifically to `/api` endpoints in `src/server.js`, wrapping routes handled by `src/routes/sessions.js`.\n- **Proxy Configuration:** Configure Express with `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` in `src/server.js` so that client IP detection correctly resolves the client rather than internal Docker network interfaces or reverse proxy containers (as defined in `docker-compose.yml`).\n- **Response Contract:** Configure the middleware to emit standard HTTP 429 status codes with standard headers (`Retry-After`, `RateLimit-Limit`, `RateLimit-Remaining`, and `RateLimit-Reset`).\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Missing Dependencies:** `package.json` currently only lists `cors`, `dotenv`, `express`, `ignore`, and `pg`. Adding `express-rate-limit` will require updating `package.json` and verifying build steps in `Dockerfile`.\n- **Route Scoping in `src/server.js`:** Ensure the middleware is attached specifically to `app.use('/api', ...)` or directly on `src/routes/sessions.js`, ensuring static routes like `express.static('public')` remain unaffected.\n- **In-Memory Volatility Across Replicas:** The default in-memory store resets counters whenever the Node.js process restarts and does not synchronize state across multiple Docker containers or cluster instances.\n- **NAT & Shared IP Throttling:** Using IP-based identification may aggregate traffic for distinct end users behind corporate proxies or NAT gateways, resulting in false-positive throttles.\n- **Prompt Token Discrepancies:** Limiting request quantity does not limit request size; a single request can still submit maximum-context prompts to `src/llm/index.js`, consuming disproportionate token budgets within the allowed request count.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- **Keying Strategy:** Should client identification be based on IP address (`req.ip`) or a session token passed from the client, given that IP-based keying requires proxy trust settings in Docker?\n- **Storage Mechanism:** Should rate limit state rely on an in-memory store for initial simplicity, or should it leverage the existing PostgreSQL database (`src/db/index.js`, `src/db/schema.sql`) or a Redis instance to persist across container restarts and scale horizontally?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Nominal:** 2–4 days\n- **Worst Case:** Up to 8 days (if persisting rate limit records to PostgreSQL and handling distributed edge cases)."}